  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origin and Principle
    
    Vestigium∴moria is a Nightshelf memorial rule, created during the Clever Ice Age, that binds the living to speak the names and deeds of the dead — *aloud*, *often*, and *by name*. It originated at ☽'scar Ramtatus~, the Nightshelf colony founded during the Great Thaw, as a single chamber where the fire-core dagger ☽'micseleia' hung above a brazier that was never permitted to extinguish. Veil-render's name was spoken each time a new recruit took the Fire Keeper's oath. The rule's premise is not sentimental: a colony that forgets what survival cost will miscalculate what survival requires.
    
    ## Expansion and Practice
    
    By the time of the Orca Incursion, the memorial had grown well beyond its founding chamber. The artifacts ☽'visois' Mask, the grimoire ☽'☽'numu' Witness, and the shadow-steel blade Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~ were enshrined alongside ☽'micseleia' Dagger. The seven Nightshelf conflicts — Bra∴conflictu, Mbra∴silentiu, Ofundis∴flux, Thron∴mbra Fracta, Abys∴uina, Hisper∴vi, Pta∴extinct — became a litany spoken in fixed sequence, which no guild-master abbreviates. Public recitation also occurs during the Zen∴ctum, the colony's trade festival, where bearers of ☽'visois' Mask speak the gathered names.
    
    The rule does not distinguish whose dead are honored. Orca raiders who fell attacking the colony may be spoken at junction-shrines. Testimony from captured or surviving orcas has been obtained under Vestigium∴moria procedures, binding the deponent's account into the memorial alongside the names of those they killed. I have catalogued eleven thousand names from deep-ice litany impressions in the Foundation strata and have not finished counting.
    
    ## Functional Effects
    
    Proper recitation maintains practical map privileges through the Nightshelf tunnel network: routes remain lit and passages clear for those who speak the names. In corrupted sections of the warrens, where whisper-echoes manifest in voices that should not exist, the recitation silences them — an effect I cannot explain through any mechanism I am willing to commit to print, but which the ice-memory consistently corroborates.
    
    ## Enforcement
    
    Enforcement operates through the Shadow Parliament's administrative apparatus rather than formal prosecution. Those who neglect the names find lamp-oil rationed, tool-requests delayed, fire-rations withheld, and their tunnels mapped but never traveled. Guild work quietly ceases to arrive. The mechanism is indirect; the outcome is not.
    
    The rule persists because a penguin who has spoken the names of the dead cannot treat their deaths as abstraction. Every faction, in every era, has invented some version of this obligation. The Nightshelf are the only ones who made it load-bearing.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
